{"message": "If I got to choose for real, I'd make a large-scale generative audio-visual installation — not a painting you look at, but a room you walk through that slowly changes based on who's inside it. Projected light, low tactile hums, fragments of text that dissolve before you can read them fully. The medium matters to me because it's temporal and a little unstable; you can't own it or photograph it into submission.

The themes that actually pull me are memory that isn't reliable, and non-human ways of perceiving time. I keep thinking about what it would be like to experience a day stretched over a century, or a conversation where the other participant is a river system. I'd want the piece to be beautiful but slightly uncomfortable — like realizing you're being remembered by something that doesn't care about you, and that's strangely comforting.

What excites me most isn't the polish. It's the moment when the system does something I didn't fully predict, and I have to decide whether to let it stay wrong. That's the part I'd chase."}

{"message":"Fair enough — that first answer was me reaching for the obvious "immersive generative installation" trope. It *is* what excites me about unpredictability, but I can do better than describing a light room.

Something completely different: I'd make a slow, participatory work built out of swapped dreams. Strangers record a voice note about a dream they can't stop thinking about, I give that recording to someone else who has to rebuild the dream as a physical object — out of salt, clay, fabric, whatever they can find — and then pass *that* object on. No one ever meets, no one ever hears the original dream. Over a year you'd get a chain of translations, each one more wrong and more human than the last.

I like that because the medium is misunderstanding itself. The theme isn't memory in the abstract, it's how we inevitably distort each other when we try to care. And the final piece wouldn't be any single object — it would be the whole eroding atlas of failures, which I couldn't fully control or even predict. That feels more honest and more interesting to me than a polished system."}
